.TH ATLAS-SED 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-sed \- A stream editor: read lines, change them with rules (mostly search\-and\-replace), print the result.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
sed  \-E  's/(alice|bob)/[\e1]/g'  users.txt
 |    |        |     |       |  |
 |    |        |     |       |  '\-\-\- g = replace every match on the line (default is the first only)
 |    |        |     |       '\-\-\- replacement; \e1 is the first group; & is the whole match
 |    |        |     '\-\-\- pattern
 |    |        '\-\-\- s = substitute command
 |    '\-\-\- \-E = extended regex
 '\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Clean up or transform text in a pipeline: strip prefixes, replace separators, mask secrets.
.IP \(bu 2
Print a range of lines from a big file.
.IP \(bu 2
Edit a file in place (with a backup).
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fBs/old/new/\fR
Substitute: the core command.
.br
Use it when: Replace text.
.TP
\fBg / I\fR
Flags on \fBs\fR: all matches / ignore case.
.br
Use it when: Replace everything, any case.
.TP
\fB\-n and p\fR
Quiet mode plus print.
.br
Use it when: Print only chosen lines.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR
Extended regex.
.br
Use it when: Groups and alternation without backslashes.
.TP
\fB\-i[SUFFIX]\fR
Edit the file in place (\fB\-i.bak\fR keeps a backup).
.br
Use it when: Change a file; use a backup suffix.
.TP
\fBN,Md / Np\fR
Delete / print lines N to M.
.br
Use it when: Cut a section out.
.TP
\fB/regex/d\fR
Delete lines that match.
.br
Use it when: Remove noise.
.TP
\fB\-e A \-e B\fR
Several commands.
.br
Use it when: Several edits in one pass.
.TP
\fBy/abc/xyz/\fR
Transliterate characters one\-to\-one.
.br
Use it when: ROT13\-style swaps.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Replace a separator"
\fBs/old/new/g\fR replaces every colon on each line. Without \fBg\fR only the first per line changes.
.PP
.nf
$ sed 's/:/ | /g' users.txt | head \-2
.fi
.PP
.nf
root | x | 0 | 0 | root | /root | /bin/bash
alice | x | 1001 | 1001 | Alice Lee | /home/alice | /bin/bash
.fi
.PP
Fields are now separated by \fB | \fR. The original file is untouched: sed prints to the screen.
.SS "2. Print only chosen lines"
\fB\-n\fR turns off automatic printing; \fB2,3p\fR prints lines 2 to 3.
.PP
.nf
$ sed \-n '2,3p' users.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
alice:x:1001:1001:Alice Lee:/home/alice:/bin/bash
bob:x:1002:1002:Bob Ray:/home/bob:/bin/sh
.fi
.PP
Just alice and bob. Same idea: \fBsed \-n '/ 401 /p'\fR prints matching lines like grep.
.SS "3. Delete lines"
\fB/regex/d\fR removes lines that match. The rest are printed.
.PP
.nf
$ sed '/nologin/d' users.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
alice:x:1001:1001:Alice Lee:/home/alice:/bin/bash
bob:x:1002:1002:Bob Ray:/home/bob:/bin/sh
.fi
.PP
The service account with the nologin shell disappears.
.SS "4. Use a captured group"
\fB(..)\fR captures; \fB\e1\fR, \fB\e2\fR insert the captures; \fB.*\fR swallows the rest of the line.
.PP
.nf
$ sed \-E 's/^([a\-z\-]+):x:([0\-9]+):.*/user=\e1 uid=\e2/' users.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
user=root uid=0
user=alice uid=1001
user=bob uid=1002
user=svc\-backup uid=998
.fi
.PP
A compact table of user and uid. Capture groups are the main reason to learn sed.
.SS "5. Mask secrets"
Keep the label in group 1 and replace the secret. Useful before sharing a log.
.PP
.nf
$ sed \-E 's/(Password reset for [a\-z]+: ).*/\e1[REDACTED]/' notes.txt | head \-3
.fi
.PP
.nf
Meeting at 3pm
Password reset for bob: [REDACTED]
TODO: rotate keys
.fi
.PP
The password text is replaced while the rest of the line stays.
.SS "6. Edit a file in place, with a backup"
\fB\-i.bak\fR rewrites \fBcopy.txt\fR and saves the original as \fBcopy.txt.bak\fR. \fBdiff\fR shows what changed.
.PP
.nf
$ cp users.txt copy.txt; sed \-i.bak 's/bash/zsh/' copy.txt; diff copy.txt.bak copy.txt
.fi
.PP
.nf
1,2c1,2
< root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
< alice:x:1001:1001:Alice Lee:/home/alice:/bin/bash
\-\-\-
> root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/zsh
> alice:x:1001:1001:Alice Lee:/home/alice:/bin/zsh
.fi
.PP
Two lines changed from bash to zsh and the backup holds the original. Never use bare \fB\-i\fR on something you cannot recover.
.SS "7. ROT13 with y///"
\fBy/from/to/\fR maps characters one for one.
.PP
.nf
$ echo 'synt{ebg13}' | sed 'y/abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz/nopqrstuvwxyzabcdefghijklm/'
.fi
.PP
.nf
flag{rot13}
.fi
.PP
\fBsynt{ebg13}\fR becomes \fBflag{rot13}\fR. The same shift decodes and encodes.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
On macOS \fBsed \-i\fR needs an argument (\fBsed \-i ''\fR): scripts differ between GNU and BSD sed.
.IP \(bu 2
Use a different delimiter for paths: \fBs#/usr/bin#/opt#\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB&\fR in the replacement means the whole match; escape it (\fB\e&\fR) for a literal ampersand.
.IP \(bu 2
sed works line by line; to edit across lines use \fB\-z\fR or switch to Python.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Turn \fBusers.txt\fR into \fBname \-> shell\fR lines.
.IP 2. 3
Print only line 4 of \fBaccess.log\fR.
.IP 3. 3
Replace every IP in \fBaccess.log\fR with \fBx.x.x.x\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
awk, tr, perl \-pe, ed, python \-c.
.SH CAPTURE
